package pane:extension@0.1.0;

// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 OR MIT
//
// The system's clipboard, opening things, the file manager and the Recycle
// Bin (ADR 0037), and pasting into the application that was in front
// before Pane, that application and the text selected in it: every command
// imports them, whatever its mode, through the `extension` world
// (wit/extension.wit). Rust commands use them through pane-guest
// (`pane_guest::system`, and the standard actions of
// `pane_guest::actions`), JavaScript and TypeScript ones through
// `@pane/extension/system` or the module "pane:extension/system@0.1.0"
// (guests/js/system.d.ts). Each does only what it names: none closes the
// window or tells the user anything, but `paste`, which closes it by
// nature. The SDKs' standard actions (Copy, Paste, Open, Open With…, Show
// in Explorer, Move to Recycle Bin) compose them with `window.close` and a
// HUD or a toast (wit/feedback.wit). None is a
// reason to pause an extension: a refusal is an answer, and the time spent
// in them (a handler starting, the clipboard held by another program) is
// not the guest's computing. The header follows `package`: comments above
// it would document the package, which extension.wit already does.

/// The system Pane runs on, as a command reaches it through Pane.
interface system {
  /// What is put on the clipboard, or read from it.
  variant clip {
    /// Text.
    text(string),
    /// A file or folder, by its absolute path, as copying it in the file
    /// manager puts it there.
    file(string),
  }

  /// The system Pane runs on, for naming things as it does ("Show in
  /// Explorer", "Show in Finder").
  enum host-system {
    windows,
    macos,
    linux,
    /// Another system.
    other,
  }

  /// A path `trash` did not move, and why.
  record not-trashed {
    path: string,
    reason: string,
  }

  /// The system Pane runs on.
  running-on: func() -> host-system;

  /// Puts `content` on the system clipboard, replacing what was there.
  /// With `concealed`, the copy carries each system's "do not record"
  /// marker where one exists (on Windows the formats that keep it out of
  /// clipboard monitors and of Windows' clipboard history, on macOS the
  /// concealed pasteboard type), so clipboard managers, Pane's own
  /// clipboard history among them, do not keep it. An error says why the
  /// system did not take it.
  copy: func(content: clip, concealed: bool) -> result<_, string>;

  /// What the clipboard holds now: its text, or the file copied in the
  /// file manager (the first, when several were), or none when it holds
  /// neither (it is empty, or holds only an image, say). An error says why
  /// Pane could not read it, such as that it cannot on this system yet.
  read-clipboard: func() -> result<option<clip>, string>;

  /// Opens `target`, unfiltered, as Raycast does: a URL of any scheme
  /// (`https:`, `mailto:`, `ms-settings:`, an application's own), any file
  /// or folder, or an application (programs included), by its path or by
  /// the id `applications.installed` gives it. It opens with the system's
  /// handler, or with `application` when one is given, by its path or by
  /// its installed-application id. The command is handed off to the
  /// system as the user's own opening would be, and is not waited for. An
  /// error says why the system did not open it.
  open: func(target: string, application: option<string>) -> result<_, string>;

  /// Shows the file or folder `path` selected in File Explorer (the
  /// system's file manager elsewhere). An error says why it could not,
  /// such as that the path does not exist.
  reveal: func(path: string) -> result<_, string>;

  /// Moves each of `paths` to the Recycle Bin (the system's trash
  /// elsewhere), from where the user can put it back. Answers the paths it
  /// could not move, each with why; a path that does not exist is one of
  /// them.
  trash: func(paths: list<string>) -> result<_, list<not-trashed>>;

  /// Why `paste`, `front-application` or `selected-text` did not answer.
  variant system-error {
    /// Pane cannot do this on this system yet: on macOS and Linux (X11
    /// included), and on Windows until Pane's Windows power features land.
    /// Nothing went wrong, and the command can do something else, as the
    /// SDKs' Paste copies instead. Says what is not available, for the
    /// user.
    not-available(string),
    /// It went wrong: why, for the user.
    failed(string),
  }

  /// The application that was in front before Pane.
  record front-app {
    /// Its name, as the system shows it ("Notepad").
    name: string,
    /// What its icon is the system's icon of: its program's path, its
    /// bundle or its desktop entry, or a Windows `shell:AppsFolder` name.
    /// An item shows it as a file icon (`{"file": …}` in the tree). None
    /// when Pane knows no such thing.
    icon: option<string>,
  }

  /// Closes the window (as `window.close` does, in a call a window was
  /// shown for), brings the application that was in front before Pane
  /// back to the front and pastes `content` into it, then puts back what
  /// the clipboard held before, unless something else was copied
  /// meanwhile. The paste goes through the clipboard: `content` is put
  /// there concealed, so clipboard managers do not keep it, and so is
  /// what is put back. `not-available` leaves the window and the
  /// clipboard as they were; the SDKs' Paste then copies instead and
  /// says so.
  paste: func(content: clip) -> result<_, system-error>;

  /// The application that was in front before Pane (its name and icon),
  /// or none (the desktop, say). It lets an action say "Paste to
  /// Notepad".
  front-application: func() -> result<option<front-app>, system-error>;

  /// The text selected in the application that was in front before Pane,
  /// or none when nothing is selected there, which is not a failure.
  selected-text: func() -> result<option<string>, system-error>;
}
